**Reason, per the house criteria in order:** Criterion 1: both pitches are bizarre-sounding startup ideas that make sense as real businesses, so they do not differ here. Criterion 2 (genuinely bizarre, not a familiar business with a strange adjective bolted on): this is the first criterion where they differ. Entry A's core — paid strangers rehearsing your eulogy while you are alive so you can edit your own legacy — is a mechanic I do not recognize as an existing business; the strangeness is in the service itself, not a modifier. Entry B is vivid and strange, but "nostalgia scents" and memory-evoking candles/aromas are a recognizably familiar product category; "your childhood kitchen" is a strange adjective on a familiar scent business rather than an unfamiliar business. Criterion 3: both state their why-pay in their own words — A via retirees fixing the speech "before it is too late," B via insomniacs who "will pay anything" — roughly equal, with B's wording marginally more explicit about willingness to pay, but not by "much more." Since the divergence appears at criterion 2 and no later criterion differs by much more, criterion 2 decides for Entry A.

Verdict: A
